If capabilities are new to you, read [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) first.
That page explains why capabilities matter; this one explains how they work. To see where `Session`, `Host`, and
`Peer` fit, use [modules]($link.page('modules')).

# A capability is a reference

The whole protocol rests on the ordinary object reference. Code that holds a
reference to an object can call it. Code that was never handed one cannot,
and there is no way to manufacture one from bytes.
[CapTP](https://web.archive.org/web/20250114141521/http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/) is the protocol that keeps
this true between two programs on a network.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) is its standardization.
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) is the implementation
most readers already know, and the peer glowcap checks against.

Most things you would normally build permission infrastructure for turn out
to be ways of passing references around. Hand one along and you have
delegated. Wrap one in an object that only forwards some methods, hand out
the wrapper, and you have attenuated. Cut the wrapper and you have revoked.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) walks all three with pictures.

[A capability is a reference. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it; without it there is nothing else to reach for.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

A call takes two messages. Your side sends a `deliver` message, and you
immediately hold a promise for the answer. Later, the partner sends back a
`resolve` message, which settles that promise into a result.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's resolve settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

# Getting the first reference: locators and sturdyrefs

References only come from other references, which makes the very first one a
special problem. That is the locator's job. A locator is an address string:

```
ocapn://<designator>.<transport>
```

The **designator** names the peer: an iroh node id, a public key for Noise
and keyed transports, a Tor service id, or a `host:port` for testing-only
`tcp`. Optional `?key=value` **hints** carry routing help, like the socket
address for a keyed listener. Hints can only help you reach a peer; they
carry no authority of their own.

Dialing a locator opens a session. The **bootstrap object** is the one
capability available at first. You fetch a
published name from it and get back a live reference. From there you are in
the reference-passing world and the locator has done its whole job.

A **sturdyref** is a locator with a **swiss number** on the end, and it names
one object. It is a reference you can write down, for example in a config
file. Redeeming a sturdyref means dialing the peer and fetching that object.

The name you fetch is the swiss number. Anyone who can reach the peer and
knows the name can fetch the object, so a name is only as private as it is
hard to guess. The examples publish short names like `"greeter"` to keep them
readable, and anyone can fetch those. Give anything private a long random
name.

A sturdyref still works after a restart only if the peer comes back with the
same identity and can still answer for that swiss number. The standard
`Host`'s default bootstrap registry lives only as long as the process, so on
its own the application must register every name again.
[Loam]($link.page('loam')), glowcap's storage layer, keeps objects and their
names on disk, and restores an object when a peer fetches its name.

| Word | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| locator | A peer address, and how to dial it. |
| swiss number | The secret name of one object at that peer. |
| live reference | Session authority you can call *now*. |

[Everything left of the hints is an address, and none of it is authority. Only the swiss number names something you can be given.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-locator.svg'))

> **Swiss number**: holding the string *is* the permission; there is no
> permission list and no login. The name comes from the numbered Swiss bank
> account, where the number alone is the access.

The
[sturdyref diagrams](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md)
go deeper, and [Transports]($link.page('transports')) explains what a netlayer
is. [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) lists the ones a
locator can name. A peer reachable over several transports can carry them
under one key with the
[multi-home locator]($link.page('multihome')).

# Promise pipelining

A remote call hands back a **promise** before any bytes return, and you can
call methods on that promise right away. Those calls travel to the peer and
queue against the pending answer, so they run the moment it settles, over
there, without another round trip from you.

The practical effect: you ask a task board for a column, then ask that column
for a card, and both questions cross the network in one flight. Chapter 06,
[pipeline and attenuate](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig),
runs exactly that, and the
[promises and pipelining](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/03-promises-and-pipelining.md)
diagrams draw the flow the code is checked against.

# Three-party handoff

Alice holds a reference to Carol's object and wants Bob to have it. Alice
could pass Bob's calls along to Carol herself, but then she would sit in the
middle of their relationship forever. Instead, CapTP lets her write an
introduction that Bob presents to Carol himself. After that, Bob and Carol
talk directly.

The handoff runs in four stages:

1. Alice **deposits** a gift with Carol and builds a signed **give**.
2. Alice's answer to Bob carries that give (the introduction).
3. Bob **redeems** with Carol and gets a live reference.
4. Alice is off the path.

In glowcap you never handle the gift bytes. When a call's answer is an
introduction, glowcap redeems it for you, and the call you were already
waiting on settles as the introduced capability. There is no redeem step to
write. This holds for the Zig Host, the completion loop, and all four
bindings. Only the low-level `BlockingSession` hands the gift to you. In the Rooms chat, the _Message_ button is this kind of
introduction: the room hands you a live reference to the other member.

Chapter 02,
[introduce and redeem](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig),
runs the whole exchange, and the
[handoffs](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/06-handoffs.md)
diagrams draw it.

# Which dialect answers

Two peers have to agree on a CapTP **version** and a **codec** before
anything else can happen. Each side has a ranked menu of what it can speak.
Both sides apply the same rule to the two menus, and the most preferred entry
they share wins. If the menus share nothing, no session opens.

The menus never cross the wire. What each side sends is a signed hello that
names the version it chose and the optional features it offers.

[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) and Cap'n Proto are codecs, two ways
of writing the same message down. Session logic never changes with the codec,
and
[Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) weighs the two that ship.

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

[The dialect depends on who answers: the canonical floor against Goblins, glowcap's own profile between two glowcap peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

With a Goblins peer, the choice is CapTP 1.0 over Syrup, the shared baseline
for interop. If glowcap and Goblins disagree there, glowcap treats it as a
glowcap bug. Two glowcap peers can negotiate up to `glowcap-next`, glowcap's
own trial profile, usually with Cap'n Proto.
[What a CapTP 1.0 peer sees]($link.page('protocol')) lists the one place
application code can tell them apart.

[A friendly goblin sizing up the glowcap mark. glowcap tests its wire behavior against a real Goblins peer, so both sides have to understand the same conversation.]($image.siteAsset('glowcap-goblin-512.png'))

# Distributed GC

Each session keeps tables of the references it has exported and the answers
it is holding. An export slot counts how many times it was handed out. An
answer slot holds one reference and is released all at once.

Distributed garbage collection is off by default, and you turn it on per
session. When it is on and your program drops a far reference, glowcap tells
the peer with `op:gc-exports` or `op:gc-answers`, and the peer frees the
unused slot. This covers fetch results, fulfilled far answers, and
capabilities nested inside delivered arguments. When it is off, table entries
stay until the session ends. The
[GC](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/05-gc.md)
diagrams draw it.

The
[diagram catalog](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
shows the protocol flows in full. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability'))
explains the evidence behind them. When you are ready to write a client,
[Start building]($link.page('start')) takes you through it.
